<h2 id="about">What this tool does</h2>
<p>Splitting a PDF means turning one long document into two or more smaller ones. The reasons are usually practical: an examiner wants only chapter 3, a bank portal refuses attachments over 5 MB, or each payslip in the yearly bundle must go out as its own file. This page reads your document with pdf-lib in the browser and writes the requested ranges as separate downloads, so nobody else ever sees the contents.</p>
<p>You describe what you want in plain page arithmetic. 'Every page separately' produces one file per page named with its number. 'Fixed chunks' groups, say, 10 pages at a time. 'Custom ranges' takes the notation you already know — 1-3, 7, 12-15 — and creates one PDF for each range you list. Ranges can overlap and do not need to be in order; 5-8,1-4 is a quick way to reorder a two-part document without merging anything.</p>
<p>Because splitting copies page objects instead of repainting them, the pages that come out are byte-for-byte the same quality as the ones that went in: fonts, links and vector drawings survive, and text stays searchable. A ZIP button collects all the pieces into a single archive so the download bar does not fill with twelve files.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-use">How to use the Split PDF <span style="color:var(--cy)">| Step by Step Guide</span></h2>
<div class="howto"><ol><li><span>Drop a .pdf into the box; the page count appears beside the file name as soon as it is read.</span></li><li><span>Pick a mode: one file per page, fixed chunks of N pages, or custom ranges.</span></li><li><span>For custom ranges type the pages you want, separated by commas — for example 1-4, 9, 12-16.</span></li><li><span>Press 'Split PDF' (pdf-lib streams from the CDN on the first click only).</span></li><li><span>Download each piece from the results list, or press 'Download ZIP' for all of them at once.</span></li></ol></div>
<span class="ru" data-ru lang="ros">PDF box me dal dein, phir mode chunein — har page alag file, barabar chunks (jaise 10-10 pages), ya apna range jaise 1-4,9,12-16. 'Split PDF' dabate hi chhoti files ban jati hain. Sab process aapke device par, file kabhi upload nahi hoti.</span>
<h2 id="features">Features</h2>
<ul class="feat-list"><li>Three modes: one-per-page, fixed-size chunks, or typed custom ranges</li><li>Comma ranges support single pages and pairs together (1-3, 7, 12-15)</li><li>Reorder-free copy keeps vector text, links and fonts intact</li><li>Results listed with page counts and file sizes before you save</li><li>One ZIP for all parts when a split produces many files</li><li>Works on protected-free files of 50+ pages without a timeout</li></ul>
<h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
<ul><li>Check the page count first — PDF viewers sometimes number cover pages differently from the tool, which is why ranges land off by one.</li><li>If a portal caps attachments, split into chunks and lower the chunk size until the largest piece fits; page count alone does not predict size.</li><li>For a yearly bundle of identical payslips, 'one file per page' plus a rename pass is faster than hunting each month manually.</li><li>Keep the original untouched: split output is a new file, so a wrong range is cheap to redo.</li><li>Very large images inside a few pages usually cause size problems — those pages are better converted with PDF to Images.</li></ul>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<details><summary>What range notation does it accept?</summary><p>Hyphens for spans and commas to separate them: 1-4, 9, 12-16. Spaces are ignored and out-of-order entries are allowed; anything outside the document's page count is reported before you press split.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will the split files be the same quality?</summary><p>Yes — pages are copied, not re-rendered, so a split file opened next to the original looks identical and searches the same.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is the split pdf free to use?</summary><p>Yes — Split PDF has no usage cap, no sign-up and no watermark, and it is not a trial: the page is a static file on https://wafasmart.tech with no billing backend behind it. </p></details>
<details><summary>Does anything get uploaded when I use split pdf?</summary><p>No. Split PDF reads your PDF straight into page memory, works on it locally and hands the result back as a download — there is no upload request to intercept, store or leak, and no log of what you processed.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I split a 200-page PDF?</summary><p>Yes, if your device has memory for it. Most laptops manage a few hundred pages; if a tab crashes, first use Extract PDF Pages to halve the file, then split the halves.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does it ask me to download an engine?</summary><p>The page is a normal static file. The pdf-lib code (about 0.5 MB) is fetched from jsDelivr with a fixed version and integrity hash the first time you press Split, then reused from cache.</p></details>
